TITLE:: RLWreath
summary:: Uniform transformations (Hook): a transposition for each chord type, and a permutation of the types
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLExtension, Classes/RLChordContext, Classes/RLTransform, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
A uniform transformation moves every chord of one type by the same interval and changes its type by a fixed rule. An element is code::[shifts, types]::: a chord of type i goes to the root code::shifts[i]:: higher, with type code::types[i]::. For two types and 12 steps these are Hook's 288 uniform triadic transformations, written <p major, p minor, sign>; for k types and n steps they form the wreath product of Z_n by the permutations of k things, n^k k! elements.

table::
## P || code::[[0, 0], [1, 0]]:: || <0, 0, ->
## L || code::[[4, 8], [1, 0]]:: || <4, 8, ->
## R || code::[[9, 3], [1, 0]]:: || <9, 3, ->
## T5 || code::[[5, 5], [0, 1]]:: || <5, 5, +>
::

Inversions are not uniform: their shift depends on the root. One uniform transformation repeated makes a progression: Beethoven's Ninth alternates R and L, which is <9, 8, -> again and again. The right action of each of the eight groups of link::Classes/RLExtension:: is a set of 24 uniform transformations.

code::
~w = RLWreath(12, 2);
~space = RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(12));  ~ctx = RLChordContext(~space);
~w.of(~ctx.l, ~space);                 // [[4, 8], [1, 0]]
~w.of(~ctx.i(0), ~space);              // nil
~u = [[9, 8], [1, 0]];
~w.act(~u, ~space.major(0));           // A minor
~w.mul(~u, ~u);                        // [[5, 5], [0, 1]]: twice is a fourth up
~w.order(~u);                          // 24: it meets every triad
~w.size;                               // 288
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: n
The number of steps of the division.
argument:: k
The number of chord types.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: n
METHOD:: k

METHOD:: size
n^k k!.

METHOD:: identity
METHOD:: elements
The element that moves nothing; every element (for small n and k).

METHOD:: mul
u after v.

METHOD:: inv
METHOD:: order

METHOD:: act
What an element makes of a chord of a k-type space.

METHOD:: swap
The element that exchanges the types i and j, a chord of type i going code::shift:: higher and a chord of type j as much lower; chords of the other types stay. The shifts of such an element sum to zero.

METHOD:: census
The swaps one voice makes by itself, as code::[i, j, shift]:: with i below j: a chord of type i and the chord of type j whose root is code::shift:: higher differ by one voice, moved by one of code::moves:: steps (1 or 2 when not given). Chords are told apart by root and type, so a chord that is its own transposition counts once for each root it can be given. For the five types of link::Classes/RLChordSpace#*sevenths:: these are the 17 operations of Cannas, Antonini and Pernazza (P12, R12, L13... Q43, N51); as moves of notes they are 11.
code::
~w = RLWreath(12, 5);  ~sevenths = RLChordSpace.sevenths;
~w.census(~sevenths);                                  // 17 triples
~l13 = ~w.swap(0, 2, 4);                               // dominant <-> half-diminished a major third up
~w.act(~l13, ~sevenths.chord(0, \dom7));               // E hdim7: one voice, C to D
~w.mul(~w.swap(1, 3, 3), ~w.swap(1, 3, 8));            // [[0, 5, 0, 7, 0], [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]]: no type changed
::

METHOD:: generated
Everything a list of elements generates, the identity first; nil past code::max:: elements (for small groups: the four type swaps without shift generate the 120 permutations of five types).

METHOD:: of
The element that does what a function of chords does on a space of an RLEDO, or nil when the function is not uniform.
argument:: func
A Function or an link::Classes/RLTransform::, chord -> chord.
argument:: space
